course: technology
lesson: 39
title: Robots at work and at home
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A robot is a machine that senses the world, decides what to do by following its program, and then moves. A robot vacuum feels a chair leg with its bumper, its program says turn, and its wheels turn; that sense, decide, move loop is the heart of every robot, from a toy to a Mars rover.
explain: In a car factory, big robot arms weld and paint the same way thousands of times without getting tired or bored, so they work behind fences where people cannot wander in. Smaller cobots are built to work beside people and stop the moment they touch someone.
explain: Robots also go where people should not: a bomb-disposal robot rolls up to a suspicious bag while the police stay far away, and a rover drives on Mars, where no person has ever stood.
explain: Even a smart robot is not a person and can make mistakes. A surgical robot moves only when the surgeon moves its controls, and a robot lawnmower does not see a hedgehog in the dark, so people stay in charge and stay careful.
fact: A factory robot is controlled by a computer that drives motors in each joint, and sensors and cameras help it pick, place, weld or paint the same way every time; big ones work behind fences or slow down and stop near people.
fact: A cobot, or collaborative robot, is a smaller robot made to work beside people; it senses touch and stops if it bumps someone, and it can be taught by moving its arm by hand.
fact: A robot vacuum uses sensors, and sometimes a spinning laser or a camera, to map the room, bumps gently into things and returns to its dock to charge; pick up small toys, cables and socks before it runs.
fact: A robot lawnmower stays inside a hidden wire or uses GPS and cuts a little grass every day; keep children and pets away, never pick it up while it is running, and run it in the day, because it can hurt hedgehogs at night.
fact: A delivery drone carries a small parcel, flies a planned route with GPS and lowers the parcel down; some countries use drones to deliver medicines to remote places.
fact: Pavement delivery robots are small robots on wheels that use cameras and sensors to drive along pavements; in Milton Keynes six-wheeled ones deliver groceries, and people should give them space and never climb on them.
fact: Warehouse robots carry whole shelves or boxes to human pickers, following codes on the floor, and in some warehouses robots zip around on a grid picking boxes from above.
fact: In robotic surgery the surgeon sits at a console and controls the robot arms, which make very small, steady movements; the surgeon is always in charge and the robot does not decide.
fact: A bomb-disposal robot is a tracked robot with cameras and an arm, driven by remote control, so people can stay far away from danger.
fact: Never touch a suspicious object or bag; move away and tell an adult.
fact: A Mars rover drives itself short distances, takes photos and tests rocks, because radio signals take minutes to travel between Mars and Earth, too slow to steer it with a joystick.
fact: The rover Perseverance carried a small helicopter called Ingenuity, which flew on Mars, the first powered flight on another planet.
fact: Robots take over repetitive tasks, but people still design, build, fix and run them.
exercise: What three things does every robot do, over and over? | Sense, decide by its program, and move | Eat, sleep and talk, just like people do | Paint, weld and sing songs while it works | Charge, sleep and then wake up when it is told
exercise: Why do big factory robots work behind fences? | Their strong fast arms could hurt a person who walks in | The fences stop them running away | Fences help them charge their batteries | Robots are shy of people
exercise: What does a cobot do if it bumps into a person? | It senses the touch and stops | It pushes harder to finish the job | It calls the police | It speeds up to get away
exercise: How can a cobot be taught a new job? | By moving its arm by hand | By shouting at it | By showing it a cartoon | It can never learn new jobs
exercise: How does a robot vacuum find its way around a room? | Sensors, and sometimes a laser or camera, map the room | It follows the smell of dust | It listens to people's footsteps | Someone drives it with a joystick
exercise: What should you do before a robot vacuum runs? | Pick up small toys, cables and socks | Pour water on the floor to help it clean | Put the cat on top of it so it can ride along and keep it company | Hide its dock so it works longer
exercise: How does a robot lawnmower know where the lawn ends? | A hidden wire or GPS marks the edge | It smells the flowers in the beds round the edge | It drives on until it bumps into a wall | It counts the blades of grass as it goes along
exercise: Why shouldn't a robot mower run at night? | It can hurt hedgehogs that come out in the dark | The grass is asleep at night | It runs out of moonlight | Its blades go blunt in the dark because the cold dew makes the metal soft
exercise: What do some countries use delivery drones for? | Delivering medicines to remote places | Carrying people to work in the morning rush | Spraying fresh paint on the walls of houses | Watching children in the playground at school
exercise: Where do six-wheeled robots deliver groceries in the UK? | Milton Keynes | On the motorway | On the River Thames | In the London Underground
exercise: How do many warehouse robots know where to go? | They follow codes on the floor | They ask the workers the way | They follow the smell of boxes | They copy each other at random
exercise: Who is in charge during robotic surgery? | The surgeon, at the console | The robot, on its own | The patient, using a remote | A nurse in another country
exercise: Why send a robot instead of a person to a suspicious bag? | So people can stay far away from danger | Robots are not allowed to be scared, so they never run off | Robots can open bags faster | People are too busy
exercise: What should you do if you see a suspicious bag left alone? | Not touch it, move away and tell an adult | Open it to see what is inside | Kick it to check if it is empty | Carry it carefully to the lost property office at the station
exercise: Why can't someone on Earth drive a Mars rover with a joystick? | Signals take minutes to travel between Mars and Earth | Joysticks are not allowed on Mars | The rover has no wheels, so it has to be lifted and carried by a crane | Mars is too dark to see the rover
exercise: What was Ingenuity? | A small helicopter that flew on Mars | A rover that drove on the Moon | A robot vacuum in space | A satellite for TV
test: «robot vacuum ate my sock again, it's out to get me» What is really going on? | It cannot tell a sock from the floor, so pick small things up first | It hunts socks on purpose | It eats socks for fuel | It is angry with you
test: «can i ride the delivery robot down the street, it's like a little car» What is the right rule? | Give it space and never climb on it | Ride it only if it is empty and going your way | Ride it only on the pavement, never on the road | Ride it if the shop that sent it says yes first
test: In a movie, a surgical robot decides on its own to do an operation at night. What is true about real ones? | The surgeon controls the arms; the robot does not decide | Surgical robots work alone at night, when the hospital is quiet and the doctors sleep | Robots choose which patient to help | Surgeons only watch from home
test: What would happen if a factory robot arm had no sensors or cameras at all? | It could not tell where things are, so it would grab and place them badly | It would work faster and better | It would sense things with its smell | Nothing, robots need no information
test: «mower's stuck under the hedge, I'll just lift it out while it's going» What is the right answer? | Never pick it up while it is running; ask an adult to switch it off | Lift it quickly by the blades | Kick it until it comes out from under the hedge, then grab it by the back | Lift it if you wear gloves
test: «found a bag on the bus seat, no one's here, open it?» What is the rule? | Don't touch it; move away and tell the driver or an adult | Open it to find the owner's name and phone number so you can ring them | Shake it to see what is inside | Take it home and keep it
test: Count them: a pavement robot has 6 wheels. How many wheels do 4 robots have? | 24 | 10 | 12 | 18
test: Count them: a car factory has 3 lines with 8 robot arms on each. How many robot arms? | 24 | 11 | 16 | 32
test: Count them: a drone delivers 2 parcels an hour. How many parcels in 5 hours? | 10 | 7 | 12 | 2
test: «robots will take every single job, there'll be nothing left for us» What is closer to the truth? | Robots take over repetitive tasks, and people still design, fix and run them | Robots already do every job | Robots cannot do any job at all | Robots only work on Mars
test: Why might a robot dog or rover be sent somewhere instead of a person, like into a broken building? | The place is too dangerous for people | Robots like dark places | People are not allowed to walk on floors | Robots are cheaper than a sandwich
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the essential operation of a robot? | It senses its surroundings, decides by means of its program and performs a movement | It eats, rests and converses | It operates exclusively at random | It requires no information concerning its surroundings
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a rover upon Mars cannot be steered in real time from the Earth. | Radio signals require several minutes to travel between the two planets | The use of controllers is prohibited upon Mars by international agreement between nations | The rover is not equipped with wheels | The surface of Mars is insufficiently illuminated
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the conduct of robotic surgery. | The surgeon controls the robotic arms from a console and remains in charge throughout | The robot selects and performs the operation without supervision | The patient directs the robot by remote control | The operation is conducted only at night
exam: Ascertain the appropriate conduct of a child who observes an unattended and suspicious bag. | The child shall not touch it, shall move away and shall inform an adult | The child shall open it in order to identify its owner | The child shall shake it in order to ascertain its contents | The child shall convey it without delay to a lost property office at the nearest station
